ling\n2922:boundaries by continuation invoke, so I1's cross-module raise test probes\n2940:- Frontend marks stay (ruling 15; decision 6 settled). Occurrence-scoped\n2962:Bundled mode (ruling 5). The bundled profile stays selectable until two\n2968:  local evaluation; the AST walker is included (ruling 6).\n2969:- The float64 address gate of section 8.5.5. Carrier preservation and\n2978:The REPL frontend catalog (ruling 16). `yin/repl.cljc` today has a\n3021:|           | content-addressed lowering-profile descriptor (ruling 17). A     |\n3029:| Support   | Explicit declarations and tests (ruling 16); reference runtime   |\n3034:The REPL `__main__` persists across submissions (ruling 18): its module\n3042:its tests (ruling 19), verifying closure origin and store ownership\n3063:|      | manifests, enforced end to end (ruling 7). P2       |                 |\n3072:|      | (ruling 6)                                          | under the       |\n3080:|      | admission (ruling 8)                                |                 |\n3082:| L-e  | D7 slice B, the origin and store check (ruling 19)  | Exposure of     |\n3111:| I1    | Static absolute imports of single modules, `py/import`, module       |\n3155:  `:segment/blake3-8f5e6bc93e0e8960682b65c2e4254ec959e69f0579ccda07a3189ea0e0e4b9d7`\n3158:  and once more, by I1's module runtime, to the address I1's entry pins\n3171:  moved once since by I1's `py` requirement pin to the address I1's\n3177:- I1: `print(\"a\"); import m; print(\"b\")` orders `a`, `m`'s output, `b`;\n3262:  `import_test`, including unchanged I1/I2 behavior and the unchanged\n3280:and I1; then I2; then I3; then I4 to I7 as their gates allow. C2-S3 to S5, C3-S3\n3405:This section records the mutable-objects ruling. A mutable guest object\n3556:  8.5.4, C3 ruling 6).\n3633:change would affect every composition (mutable-objects ruling, owner\n3642:requires and the composition installs explicitly (C3 ruling 5, section\n3819:surfaces as a deterministic guest exception (C3 ruling 11).\n3891:  guest `hash()` and integer `is` work of rulings 6 to 8 (orchestrated\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure, node, zprint, babashka, flutter 3.41.9. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version; use mise in the scratch clone to install and activate that selection before the Dart lane. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n# Track A Phase C4 Slice I1 — Static absolute imports (glm-5.3 findings)\n\nWorktree: `.claude/worktrees/yang-python-c4-i1` (base `ea247169` == master at dispatch).\nBrief: `collab/1791539186673-compiler-engineer-c4-i1.prompt.md` (copy: `collab-brief.md`).\nGoverning spec: `docs/design/yang.antlr.md` §8.5.6.\n\n## Shape landed\n\n**Runtime (`src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc`, one definition list,\nboth emitters):**\n\n- Module objects are heap cells `{:py/type :module :name s :dict d}`\n  (§8.5.6); `py/module-of`/`py/module-dict`/`py/module-new` build them,\n  `module-new` setting `__name__`, `__package__`, `__spec__`/`__loader__`\n  (None); `__file__` is absent.\n- `sys.modules` is the runtime dict `py.rt/modules`; `py/init!` pre-seeds\n  the two runtime-synthesized module objects `sys` (its dict holding\n  `py.rt/modules` under `modules`) and `builtins` (its dict IS the\n  builtins namespace, now carrying the four standard dunders, CPython's\n  own shape). `py/modules-ref`/`-put!`/`-del!` are the accessors.\n- `py/import (name spec body)` implements §8.5.6's resolution order:\n  `sys.modules` first — a hit is the module object, the body never\n  re-executes; a miss with spec/body None (a runtime-synthesized name)\n  raises the new `ImportError` builtin class; a miss instantiates via\n  `py/module-run (name package body)`, `__package__` from the spec's\n  package-ness (always None in I1).\n- `py/module-run` creates the module object, inserts it into\n  `sys.modules` BEFORE the body runs, runs `(body d globals-fn)` in the\n  importing task, and on a raising body removes the entry and re-raises\n  to the importer's handler. `py/globals-fn` factors the per-module\n  `globals` builtin.\n- `py/run-main` instantiates the SAME body image as `\"__main__\"` through\n  `module-run` and snapshots the outcome (`{:py/out … :py/exception …}`);\n  `py/run-module` keeps its test-runner role (fresh namespace, no\n  sys.modules entry). One code image therefore sees `__main__` as main\n  and its own name as an import.\n- `py/getattr`/`py/setattr` gained `:module` branches (attribute\n  read/write = dict operations on the module's own namespace, so the\n  module's functions see them; `__dict__` returns the dict itself) and\n  `py/delattr` is new (strict delete, AttributeError naming the missing\n  attribute). Module objects snapshot as `:py/module` and render as\n  `<module 'name'>`.\n\n**Lowering (`src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc`):**\n\n- `import m` (single undotted, `as`-free — I1's shape) lowers at the\n  statement to `(py/import m pym.m/spec pym.m/body)` plus the binding of\n  the module object in the scope the statement runs in; one\n  `(require 'pym.m)` is hoisted per static import in the unit beside the\n  wrapper's `py` require (ruling 4), scanned at any depth (a function\n  body import still needs delivery). An import-free program's tree is\n  byte-identical, so P2's program-root golden does not move.\n- `import sys`/`import builtins` need no delivery: spec and body are\n  `None` literals and no require is hoisted — the pre-seeded\n  `sys.modules` entry answers.\n- The bundled profile refuses any unit importing a compiled module\n  (§8.5.6: imports require the linked prelude — a module closure's free\n  reads never see the ambient store); the pre-seeded `sys`/`builtins`\n  carry no delivery, so their import lowers under either profile. The\n  pre-seeded `sys` exposes `modules` only — the interpreter hooks stay\n  unreachable (`sys.setrecursionlimit` is an AttributeError, per the\n  updated `e2e_test`). Dotted, aliased and `from` imports are refused\n  naming the construct (I2's).\n- `del_stmt` is lowered: `del m.y` is a strict delattr, `del x` reads\n  first (NameError/UnboundLocalError when unbound) then unbinds; tuple\n  targets delete each element; subscript deletes are refused as later\n  slices'.\n- The module emitter: `lower/module-packet` compiles one unit as the\n  linker module `pym.<name>` — one wide application whose operands are\n  `(require 'py)`, one `(require 'pym.<i>)` per import, then the\n  `(yin/def spec …)` and `(yin/def body (fn [%globals %globals-fn] …))`\n  definitions, exports `#{spec body}` (§8.5.6's module shape; the body\n  is the same form the main wrapper runs). `lower/module-spec` builds\n  `publish-module!`'s spec over a host registry: `py` and each import\n  pinned by manifest address in `:requires`, a free name qualified by\n  them covered by that requirement, bare names by their `vm/primitives`\n  profile, other `ns/name`s by the registry's host-export profile —\n  anything else refused `undeclared-free` before publication.\n- `lower/module-symbol` maps `m` to `pym.m` (`pym` reserved, injective,\n  nothing at the `pym` root).\n\n**Scope (`src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/scope.cljc`):** `import_stmt` binds\nthe imported names where Python does (module level: a global; a function\nbody: a local cell); `import-bindings` follows CPython's rule (`import\na.b` binds `a`, `import a.b as x` binds `x`) so I2 needs no rework.\n\n**Tests:**\n\n- `test/yang/python/antlr/import_programs.cljc` (NEW): the modules and\n  programs as CST packets, each generated from the JVM parser for its\n  docstring source, so every host runs the real lowering.\n- `test/yang/python/antlr/import_test.cljc` (NEW): fast lowering tests\n  (hoisted requires, statement-time py/import call and binding, the\n  bundled refusal, I2 refusals, scope binding, del lowering, module\n  packet/spec shape and refusals) and the slow acceptance over the\n  linked harness — all six §8.5.6 I1 rows on the semantic, stack and\n  register VMs, plus `sys.modules` identity probes, the pre-seeded\n  `sys`/`builtins` resolution, and a transitive `t`-imports-`k` run\n  (the module's install child delivers its own imports).\n- `test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_test.clj`: parser parity for every\n  import-programs packet; the bundled `import sys` refusal message\n  updated to the new linked-prelude refusal.\n- `test/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc`: `module-objects-test`\n  runs the module object protocol over the bundled prelude on all four\n  VMs (dunder read, attribute read/write/delete, `__dict__`, the\n  sys.modules hit, the None-spec ImportError); `seeded-builtins-test`\n  expects the four dunders the builtins module now carries.\n- `test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc`: `publish-guest-module!`\n  publishes a guest module's spec beside `py`.\n\n**Goldens moved once each** (the prelude definition list changed;\n§8.5.6's I1 entry records them): `py` manifest →\n`blake3-607cef06002c49a1cebdf836b751077f6d77c4618ebb317cc0533ef895d297a1`,\n`pysp` manifest (via the requirement pin) →\n`blake3-a842d2ad662f1d0a0dd7fb92922221343603472bac591073ac23ba82c9772d54`,\nbundled prelude root →\n`blake3-f4a40353e2be0a9f5c1074fb784da81c6542b8505577b8f6bdf80069203ad4e7`,\nfloat program A →\n`blake3-0fb95b9e3186be329d4127eec04d8e86143d4f83246e8c39c8177fe9acc37768`,\nA' →\n`blake3-42c5770081ef77a655db2aaedd0f6edc80269d80ae8885647883df7c1262dc81`,\nrecord →\n`blake3-c441eafa2d18d9969547ddc222d7652e23587653fb5bc9d986f8e562b0a0d9d4`,\nprelude-id →\n`blake3-bf98bc3f5fcd12e36151a1043afde9c7e14f74951176db8bd4a2a8fab28a02c6`;\n`runtime-keys` 61 → 65. The hook prelude's own root did NOT move, and the\nlinked `x = 1` program root (`dbe72b29…`) did NOT move (import-free\nwrappers are unchanged). Node computed the same bundled addresses\nbyte-for-byte.\n\n## Traps hit (for the next slice)\n\n- The prelude notation (`u/sexp->uast`) does not evaluate inside map\n  literals: `{:py/str name}` in a definition embeds the SYMBOL — the\n  module branch's dict lookups silently missed every key until the keys\n  were built with `(assoc {} :py/str name)` calls. Symptom: setattr then\n  getattr round-tripped (both used the broken key) while the module\n  body's own bindings were invisible to `m.x`.\n- `portable/packet` rules are KEYWORDS (`:file_input`); a string first\n  element is a token. The generator emitted `(:rule n)` strings; the\n  packets collapsed to single token nodes and the scope analysis saw a\n  token as the root.\n- `p/child-rules` is 3-arity `[pk node rule]`; scope's own helpers pass\n  `pk` separately — easy to drop when copying lowering idioms.\n- `del_stmt`'s single-target `exprlist` is not a tuple to\n  `target-elements` (nil); take `(or (target-elements ctx xl) [xl])`.\n- `tree-seq` over the then-chain wrapper visits requires in reverse\n  source order (operator before operand) — assert module sets, not\n  order.\n- `clojure -M:test -e …` runs the whole suite first (main-opts); use\n  `-n <ns>` or the `-v` var filter. `-A:test` and script execution are\n  approval-gated in this sandbox; `bb -e` + `clojure.java.shell/sh` is\n  the workaround for one-off commands.\n\n## Verification\n\n- clj-kondo: 0 errors, 0 warnings on every touched file (project-wide\n  baseline unchanged: pre-existing .cljd and yin/repl/host.cljc\n  findings).\n- cljstyle: clean on every touched file.\n- JVM fast, `yang.python.antlr.import-test` (`-e :slow`): 10 tests, 44\n  assertions, 0 failures.\n- JVM slow, `yang.python.antlr.import-test` (`-i :slow`): 8 tests, 30\n  assertions, 0 failures — every acceptance row on the semantic, stack\n  and register VMs.\n- JVM, `yang.python.antlr.{import,e2e}-test` fast (after the e2e fix):\n  21 tests, 221 assertions, 0 failures.\n- JVM slow, `yang.python.antlr.{linked-prelude,linked-safepoint}-test`\n  (`-i :slow`, final pins): 15 tests, 99 assertions, 0 failures — the\n  `py` and `pysp` manifest goldens, repeated-publication identity,\n  walker exclusion, cross-unit exception sharing, install-child\n  isolation.\n- Dart fast lane (`bb test:cljd`, final code): all tests passed (3778) —\n  includes the new `import-test`/`import-programs` and the parity\n  additions.\n- Full JVM fast lane (`bb test:clj`, final code): 3947 tests, 244,056\n  assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- Node fast lane (`bb test:cljs`, final code): 3826 tests, 108,231\n  assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. Node computed the new bundled\n  goldens byte-for-byte (the float-address cross-host identity the\n  release gate asks for).\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Open items / notes for the orchestrator\n\n- The report file is `./findings.md` in the worktree root (the collab\n  directory is outside this sandbox); please move it to\n  `collab/1791539186673-compiler-engineer-c4-i1.glm-5-3.findings.md`.\n- `collab-brief.md` (the staged brief copy) is untracked in the worktree\n  root; keep or drop per protocol.\n- I2's lowering groundwork is already in place: scope binds dotted/aliased\n  imports per CPython, `module-symbol` mangles `a.b.c` to `pym.a$b$c`\n  (one line when I2 lands), and `py/import`'s `__package__` derivation\n  reads the spec's `:package?`.\ncommit ce76c84607b3e950b09f66e795bcfe62229cfdea\nMerge: 6df441d08 62121c7ee\nAuthor: Sonny To <son.c.to@gmail.com>\nDate:   Fri Oct 16 13:28:32 2026 +0700\n\n    Merge srvm-module-pins (commit 1): carried install children need receiver admission; the lift never carries sender admission (fetch threat model R4; Architect signed off; JVM, Node, Dart green)\n\n docs/agents/known-gaps.md                          |  4 ++\n docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md                   |  8 ++++\n docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md        | 37 +++++++++++---\n docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md                | 10 ++++\n docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md         | 12 +++++\n .../design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | 16 +++++++\n src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc             |  8 ++--\n src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc                      | 25 +++++++++-\n test/yin/vm/semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc   | 18 ++++---\n test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export_test.cljc            | 32 ++++++++++++-\n test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc             |  4 +-\n test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc                  |  6 +++\n test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc                     | 56 ++++++++++++++++++++--\n 13 files changed, 211 insertions(+), 25 deletions(-)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2637,2688p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; sed -n '2880,2978p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; sed -n '3090,3205p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'init\"'!|linked|install|manifest|golden'\"' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/{prelude,lower}.cljc test/yang/python/antlr/{linked_harness,linked_prelude_test,float_address_test}.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2637,2688p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; sed -n '2880,2978p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; sed -n '3090,3205p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'init\"'!|linked|install|manifest|golden'\"' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/{prelude,lower}.cljc test/yang/python/antlr/{linked_harness,linked_prelude_test,float_address_test}.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"#### 8.5.6 Imports, the linked prelude, and the frontend catalog (phase C4)\n\nThis section records the C4 design as amended by the C4 cross-ruling's\nnineteen converged rulings; where they differ from the design's own\nrecommendations, the converged rulings govern. Its implementation is\npending, in slices F2, F3, P1 to P3, and I1 to I7 below; F1, P1 and P2\nhave landed; P2 covers the semantic, stack and register VMs, the walker\nwaiting on L-b.\n\nInstall delivers code; instantiation is the importing task's own\nevaluation (ruling 1). A linked Python-side module (the base prelude,\nthe hook prelude, any Python module) exports only closures and immutable\nspecification data. Its install child defines lambdas and halts. Python\nnamespaces, class objects, hook state, and runtime cells are allocated\nlater by an explicit initialization in the consuming task. This is\nsection 8.5.1's \"the linker delivers content-addressed code images,\nnever Python namespaces\" taken literally. Running a module body inside\nthe install child fails four ways against the landed linker:\n\n- The lift refuses. A heap cell ref is non-portable, and every Python\n  function, class, dict, and module is a cell, so no body-executed\n  module could reach `linked`.\n- Output vanishes. Import-time `print` writes the child's output, which\n  is never published.\n- Identities duplicate. The child lowers its own copy of each\n  dependency, so builtin classes in the export slice would be fresh cells\n  and `isinstance` would fail.\n- First link wins. The parent discards the child's writes to a\n  dependency's store.\n\nSo C4 needs no heap-slice lift, and nothing is copied per receiving task\n(section 8.5.1, amended). `py/init!` initializes the prelude's own\nactive module store once, idempotently, with defined failure behavior;\nentry wrappers call it and install children never do. An imported body\nruns under the importing task's declared effects. Statement-time delivery\nand body execution may both suspend on their explicit stream effects.\n\nModule shape. A Python unit compiled as module `a.b` becomes one linker\nmodule:\n\n```clojure\n(do (require 'py)              ; hoisted: code delivery only\n    (yin/def spec {:name \"a.b\" :package? false})\n    (yin/def body (fn [%globals %globals-fn] <lowered module body>)))\n;; :yin.module/exports #{spec body}\n```\n\n- Only `py` is eagerly required. Static guest imports remain pinned in\n  the manifest; guest delivery runs at the executed statement through\n  `try-require`, after the `sys.modules` check (ruling 8).\n- `import c` lowers to the binding plus:\n  `(py/import \"c\" (fn [] (let [r (try-require 'pym.c)]\n  which writes one state slot (`py.rt/state`, a literal `:py/uninit`\n  until `py/init!` flips it to `:py/ready` as its last write; the cells\n  and classes stay store keys the body defines).\n- The linked entry wrapper is\n  `(do (require 'py) (py/init!) (py/run-main (fn [%globals %globals-fn]\n  ...)))`. `py/run-main` is `py/run-module` until I1.\n- The lowering drops `builtin-names` and direct `py.b/*` reads, so a new\n  builtin class no longer touches the lowering, and the hand-kept host\n  name sets become derivable from the tree.\n- Imports require the linked prelude. A module closure's free reads never\n  see the ambient store, so a bundled importer's `py/*` definitions are\n  invisible to an imported body.\n\nAddresses. Every golden moves once, since the wrapper and builtin reads\nchange. Afterward a linked user program's code root stays stable across\nprelude revisions only while its emitted rows and ABI references stay\nunchanged. Its published module manifest does not: `:yin.module/requires`\nis part of the addressed manifest, so a changed prelude pin changes the\npackage identity. Code-root stability and executable package identity\nare distinct, and both the float carrier correction and the linked\nmigration preserve the distinction. The note in section 8.5.3 that each\nprelude change moves every bundled unit's address stays true of the\nbundled profile.\n\nHost-export profiles (ruling 7). Publish refuses the prelude today: a\nfree name bound by a host module is refused, and the prelude calls the\n`cell`, `data`, and later `integer` modules. Linker prerequisite L-a has\nthe prelude manifest declare its host modules (cell, data, integer, and\nstream poll) by semantic profile address, and covers publication,\nrequirement discovery, installation matching, effect declarations, and\nqualified host-export resolution, not merely a manifest field. Integer\nlimits and semantic versions are part of the matched profile identity;\nthe descriptive `module-version` (section 8.5.4) is insufficient. The\nability to execute and re-encode float64 carriers is checked without\nchanging Jing's numeric bytes or adding Python-aware codec logic.\n\nThe AST walker (ruling 6). A module whose exported lambda reads a sibling\ndefinition is refused `:undeclared-free` in the tree format, so the\nwalker cannot link the prelude today. L-b admits the linked prelude's\ndeclared sibling reads through the same verification and store-isolation\ncontract as the other evaluators. Three-VM intermediate development is\nacceptable; three-VM completion is not. Python raises across module\nboundaries by continuation invoke, so I1's cross-module raise test probes\nstore-context restoration, and D7 slice C is a prerequisite wherever that\ntest exposes incorrect restoration on any VM.\n\nSafepoints. Linked, `pysp` requires `py`, inverting today's load order.\nIts cursor cannot be created at install and a module closure cannot read\nan ambient signal stream, so the wrapper passes the stream to\n`(pysp/attach! signals)`. Landed (P3): the module emitter, exports and\npublication spec live beside the bundled emitter in\n`yang.python.antlr.safepoint`, one definition list feeding both; the\nhooks reach the cells the base prelude owns through the exports\n`py/rt-ctx` and `py/rt-limit` and the builtin classes through the\nbuiltins dict (`py.sp/class`), because `py.rt/*` and `py.b/*` are\ninternal store keys no foreign module closure can name, and the linked\nentry wrapper `linked-program` is `(require 'py)`, `(require 'pysp)`,\n`(py/init!)`, `(pysp/attach! py.sp/signals)` ahead of `A'`, whose own\nrequire and init are idempotent re-entries.\n\n- Frontend marks stay (ruling 15; decision 6 settled). Occurrence-scoped\n  marks are preserved through publication and transformation, and loop\n  or function meaning is never inferred from generic lambda structure.\n  Imported Python bodies are marked; runtime internals are excluded under\n  the selected policy.\n- Atomicity: prelude internals stay unmarked, so section 8.11's\n  runtime-internal atomicity holds, but an import runs guest code that\n  can contain effects and safepoints. The import operation as a whole is\n  not atomic.\n- Site marks live in the frontend-metadata side table, not rows, so a\n  published tree carries none. Ruling 14: keep sites outside canonical\n  rows, but publish a content-addressed site set with a pinned\n  association to the exact source root, frontend revision, and\n  transformation profile, with retrieval and verification obligations,\n  as datoms. A separate publication record may avoid a module manifest\n  schema change, conditional on complete discovery; a schema change is\n  not ruled out in advance.\n- Until site sets are published, an imported `while True` is not\n  interruptible. Uninstrumented imports are permitted only under an\n  explicitly noninterruptible profile; a profile requiring hooks refuses\n  a missing site set.\n\nBundled mode (ruling 5). The bundled profile stays selectable until two\ngates pass, then it is deleted, with no compatibility shim:\n\n- The migration gate: the linked corpus on all four evaluators on JVM,\n  Node, and Dart, twelve lanes, covering publication, parserless\n  consumption, runtime initialization, and exception parity, not merely\n  local evaluation; the AST walker is included (ruling 6).\n- The float64 address gate of section 8.5.5. Carrier preservation and\n  the execution bridge complete first; affected rows, derived images,\n  manifests, and references are then rebuilt. The linked migration\n  changes bundled roots separately, and neither transition aliases old\n  integer-misclassified content to corrected float content.\n\nThe float fix is also a hard prerequisite for P2: the prelude manifest\nmust have one address on every host.\n\nThe REPL frontend catalog (ruling 16). `yin/repl.cljc` today has a\n\n```text\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Slice | Scope                                                                |\n+=======+======================================================================+\n| F1    | `yang.frontend` catalog and manifest validation; landed in           |\n|       | `src/cljc/yang/frontend.cljc`                                        |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| F2    | The REPL selects through the catalog; `yin.repl` drops its frontend  |\n|       | requires                                                             |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| P1    | Single-source prelude, `py/init!`, builtins dict; still bundled      |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| P2    | Module emitter, publication, linked profile; needs L-a, L-f and the  |\n|       | float fix                                                            |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| P3    | `pysp` linked, `attach!`                                             |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| F3    | The Python ANTLR frontend in the catalog (JVM); persistent           |\n|       | `__main__`; needs P2                                                 |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| I1    | Static absolute imports of single modules, `py/import`, module       |\n|       | objects, `sys.modules`, `__name__`                                   |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| I2    | Packages, mangling, relative imports, `as`, `*`, `__package__`       |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| I3    | Publication and DHT import; admission per `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.5 |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| I4    | Statement-time `ModuleNotFoundError` / `ImportError` (I4-a); I4-b DHT |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| I5    | SCC units and alias modules                                          |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| I6    | Literal `import_module`, same-image `reload`; hooks refused          |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| I7    | Published site sets for imported modules                             |\n+-------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\nAcceptance, each on all three hosts unless stated (Node and Dart consume\nprecompiled CST packets or rows, since the parser is JVM-only):\n\n- F1: two revisions of one id installed into an empty catalog are both\n  selectable and the original catalog is unchanged; a manifest holding a\n  function is rejected with a qualified outcome.\n- F2: a toy frontend defined in a test namespace installs, is selected,\n  and evaluates on every VM with no `src` edit; a newer revision\n  installed under a live session leaves it unchanged; on Node and Dart\n  with no parser or configured service, the ANTLR id answers\n  unavailable-parser with no legacy fallback.\n- P1: the full corpus output is unchanged on every VM and host; a\n  composition with no cell module halts ok on `functions-uast`;\n  `py/init!` twice yields one set of class cells; shadowing then\n  deleting a builtin name makes the builtin visible again.\n- P2: `py` publishes to one pinned manifest address on each host; the\n  linked corpus output equals bundled; one source under two prelude\n  revisions keeps one program root; an exception raised in one unit is\n  `isinstance` of `Exception` read in another unit of the same task; an\n  install child at `validated` has an empty heap and no `:cell` lift\n  refusal; a wrong host-module profile is refused by name; a missing\n  name-environment entry is refused with no bundled fallback (semantic,\n  stack, register; the walker under L-b). Landed with the linked `x = 1`\n  program root at\n  `:segment/blake3-dbe72b291a26a4f594ad55cf52e5ffd088fdd262b58fd26212a01a7dd983a6df`\n  (JVM golden in `linked_prelude_test`); the `py` manifest golden the\n  slice pinned at\n  `:segment/blake3-8f5e6bc93e0e8960682b65c2e4254ec959e69f0579ccda07a3189ea0e0e4b9d7`\n  was moved once, by P3's two accessors, to\n  `:segment/blake3-b891d48b14e3fc3757535e580a3511e95d8ddf6c57d3e637f1ba0f9a97cb0ee7`\n  and once more, by I1's module runtime, to the address I1's entry pins\n  (every golden moves once per slice that changes the wrapper or the\n  definition list it addresses). Measured on the JVM: `publish-module!` of `py`\n  takes about 37 to 45 s; one linked run takes about 4.4 to 5.3 s against\n  0.4 to 1.0 s bundled, about 80% of it the CBOR decode of the served\n  image (reflective `alength` in `dao.jing.cbor/blen`).\n- P3: safepoint slice 1's acceptance re-runs under the linked profile on\n  every VM; stage input contains no prelude row; a derived program with\n  no `pysp` binding reports the unresolved hook name. Landed, with slice\n  2's recursion acceptance re-run linked too (depth tracking across the\n  module boundary), in `linked_safepoint_test`: `pysp` publishes to one\n  pinned manifest address on each host (P3 pinned\n  `:segment/blake3-6133313d60c4080c0803daa6de5b53b195fa6c650af4b4b9e354ce247950fa87`,\n  moved once since by I1's `py` requirement pin to the address I1's\n  entry names, JVM golden), and its requirement pins `py` by\n  manifest address, so a `pysp`-free name environment still runs the\n  naive linked program and refuses the derived one by hook name.\n- F3: at the REPL, `x = 1` then `print(x)` prints `1`; the probe answers\n  incomplete until a multi-line `def` closes.\n- I1: `print(\"a\"); import m; print(\"b\")` orders `a`, `m`'s output, `b`;\n  a second import does not re-execute; `m.x = 5; del m.y` is visible\n  through `m.__dict__` and to `m`'s functions; a raising body removes\n  its entry and propagates; a handler in the importer catches an\n  exception from a function in `m` on every VM; one image as main and as\n  import sees different `__name__` values. Landed in `import_test`\n  (with the programs of `import_programs`, each packet the parser's for\n  its source, checked by `e2e_test`): the runtime is `py/import`,\n  `py/module-run`, `py/run-main` as `\"__main__\"`, the module objects\n  `py/getattr`/`py/setattr`/`py/delattr` answer for, sys.modules\n  (`py.rt/modules`) with `sys` and `builtins` pre-seeded, and the\n  `ImportError` class; the module emitter is `lower/module-packet` and\n  `lower/module-spec` (`pym.m` for module `m`). I4-a replaces the\n  hoisted guest requires with statement-time `try-require` delivery\n  thunks, retaining the wrapper's eager `py` require and concrete\n  manifest pins. An import-free program's tree is unchanged, so its linked\n  program root golden did not move. Each import-free golden moved once\n  with the prelude change: the `py` manifest to\n  `:segment/blake3-ad6f34e349ce527382ddd039236197a700c6c1182f163bc3b8bcf13449f1c3d8`\n  and, through the requirement pin, the `pysp` manifest to\n  `:segment/blake3-79432fa68e23ce3d1a4fbf4f882713b8ba9fe0cb74467285cb832e25c7458da9`\n  (JVM goldens in `linked_prelude_test` and `linked_safepoint_test`);\n  the bundled prelude root, program root, derivation record and\n  prelude-id goldens in `float_address_test` moved once each; the hook\n  prelude's own root did not move. An import of a compiled module under\n  the bundled profile is refused (`Imports require the linked prelude`),\n  as is every I2 form — dotted, aliased, and `from` — and `del` is\n  lowered for name and attribute targets. `import sys` and\n  `import builtins` resolve under either profile with `None` delivery,\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:1:(ns yang.python.antlr.linked-harness\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:2:  \"The linked profile's composition for the Python tests (C4 slices P2\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:6:   every linked run over a `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` composition under\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:9:   `linked-prelude-test` asserts.\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:11:   A linked run builds the program's task on one of the three vector VMs\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:41:  \"The full host registry a linked Python composition registers: the\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:88:   `py`, so a safepointed linked run resolves both names.\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:168:(defn run-linked\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:169:  \"The finished task of the linked program `ast` on `backend`. Options:\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:175:  ([backend ast] (run-linked backend ast {}))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:186:  \"Each vector VM's runner of a linked program over `modules`: the\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:192:                    (vm/value (run-linked k ast {:modules modules})))]))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:197:  \"The linked runners under the ordinary limits.\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:1:(ns yang.python.antlr.linked-prelude-test\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:7:   until L-b. The published module and the linked runs are\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:8:   `yang.python.antlr.linked-harness`'s: one publication per process,\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:18:    [yang.python.antlr.linked-harness :as h]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:85:  (is (= 'init! (prelude/strip 'py/init!)))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:129:                '[init! run-main run-module object-class vconj lnot]))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:178:    (testing \"the runtime keys are what the generated py/init! defines,\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:182:                                       'py/init!))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:219:;; A1 and A10. `py` publishes to one pinned manifest address; the walker is\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:223:(def ^:private manifest-golden\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:231:   the rebase onto master 4529b5bd: the profile digest in the manifest\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:244:(defn- manifest-address\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:248:    (testing \"one address on every host (JVM golden)\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:249:      (is (= manifest-golden address) (pr-str (dissoc result :links :walk))))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test